\section{Translation at Reduction Time}

Donald Knuth's \textit{On the Translation of Languages from Left to Right} establishes the LR framework \cite{knuth1965lr}. The parser reads left to right and reconstructs a rightmost derivation in reverse. For a frontend, this is also a schedule for building semantic values. A shift pushes a token and its value. A reduction by $A\to X_1\cdots X_k$ takes those $k$ values in source order, computes a value for $A$, pops the corresponding entries, and pushes $A$ along with the state reached by the predecessor's goto. A zero-length reduction takes no child values and pops no entries; it still makes a goto and pushes a result.

\textit{Applications of Language Theory to Compiler Design}, by Jeffrey Ullman, explains this process in terms of handles: reducing a handle undoes the last step of a rightmost derivation. The substring alone does not identify the next reduction; its position and the surrounding parser context matter. Ullman's survey points to Franklin DeRemer's \textit{Practical Translators for LR($k$) Languages} among the early studies of practical LR construction.

\textit{Formal Languages and Compilation}, by Crespi Reghizzi, Breveglieri, and Morzenti, treats bottom-up translation alongside recognition. Anthony Aaby's \textit{Compiler Construction using Flex and Bison} shows the same relationship in parser-generator actions. A useful implementation rule is that the semantic stack and the stack of grammar symbols must describe the same prefix, even during recovery. Build nodes at a reduction, but defer changes to externally visible state until a committed phase. If recovery pops a subtree, or a generalized parser discards an alternative, neither should leave behind a declaration from a node that did not survive.

\section{Worked Witness: One Input, Two Outputs}

Let the productions for expressions be $E\to E-E\mid\mathit{num}$. Take the token stream for \texttt{10-3-2}, terminated by end of input. When the stack represents $E-E$ and the second minus is the lookahead, the completed item $E\to E-E\,\cdot$ can reduce and the item $E\to E\,\cdot\,-E$ can shift. In this grammar the completed item can carry minus as its lookahead, so the two actions compete in the same table cell.

Taking the reduction at that cell produces a subtraction with children $10$ and $3$ before the parser processes the second minus. Taking the shift there, then reducing the right pair first, produces a subtraction with children $3$ and $2$ before the outer reduction. The resulting complete trees evaluate to $(10-3)-2=5$ and $10-(3-2)=9$, respectively. They are two derivations of one terminal string, not just two possible actions in a table report. If the language requires left associativity, require the first tree; a rule such as $E\to E-\mathit{num}\mid\mathit{num}$ enforces it in this miniature language. A generator's left-associativity declaration can choose the same reduction without repairing the ambiguity of the underlying productions.

\section{When a Conflict Is Not an Ambiguity}

Some conflicts arise from the construction of the table, not from two trees for the same input. Consider the canonical example shape
\[
\begin{aligned}
 S &\to aAd\mid bAe\mid aBe\mid bBd,\\
 A &\to c,\qquad B\to c .
\end{aligned}
\]
After $ac$, the canonical LR(1) state can reduce $A\to c$ on $d$ and $B\to c$ on $e$. After $bc$, those lookaheads are reversed. The states have the same LR(0) core, so an LALR merge puts both lookaheads on both completed items and introduces reduce/reduce conflicts. The four strings $acd,ace,bcd,bce$ each still have a unique tree. Report the two distinct prefixes and their lookaheads as an explanation of the merge, not as a single ambiguity witness.

In GNU Bison 3.8.2, \texttt{-Wcounterexamples} reports both derivations for the subtraction example and gives separate examples on lookahead $d$ for the merge: $acd$ uses $A$ and $bcd$ uses $B$. Selecting \texttt{\%define lr.type canonical-lr} eliminates the merge conflicts but cannot eliminate the ambiguity of subtraction. This is a useful diagnostic comparison before rewriting productions or adding precedence declarations.

For a useful diagnostic, save the grammar items, contested lookahead, and complete token stream or streams that replay the decision. A path through parser states may include nonterminal transitions and does not by itself supply a terminal test. Expand candidate paths or search parser configurations, replay them, and see whether both actions can reach acceptance on the same input. If a bounded search finds no such input, report the bound rather than declaring the grammar unambiguous. Tests should assert the chosen action's observable result, typically tree shape; a fixed conflict count alone cannot show that the same decisions are being made. Preserving a replayable witness follows the practice proposed for compiler design in \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/32-counterexample-guided-compiler-synthesis.md}{Markdown Dossier Chapter~32}.

