\section{Inspecting Expected Tokens Without Running Actions}

A diagnostic that lists every nonerror entry in the top LR state can
overpromise: a reduction in that state might lead immediately to
an error on the same lookahead. Instead simulate the resolved table
on a copy of the current state stack. The input is that stack and
one candidate terminal, including end of input as a candidate.
The answer says whether the token can shift or trigger acceptance
after zero or more reductions. This is a local expectation, not a
promise that some entire remaining program will parse. Assume a
deterministic, unrecovered stack and a table whose reduction-only
chains terminate for the configurations tested.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input LR state stack $S$, candidate terminal $t$
  \Output whether $t$ can be taken next
  \State $W\gets\Call{Copy}{S}$ \Comment{Do not touch live values}
  \Loop
    \State $q\gets\Call{Top}{W}$
    \State $a\gets\Call{Action}{q,t}$
    \If{$a$ is shift or accept}
      \State \Return true
    \ElsIf{$a$ is error}
      \State \Return false
    \EndIf
    \State $(A,k)\gets\Call{RuleOf}{a}$
    \State \Call{PopStates}{$W,k$} \Comment{No reduction action}
    \State $q\gets\Call{Top}{W}$
    \State $g\gets\Call{Goto}{q,A}$
    \State \Call{Push}{$W,g$}
  \EndLoop
\end{sudocode}

For a trace, use the grammar $S\to a A x\mid b A y$ and $A\to c$.
An SLR table reduces $A\to c$ in the completed state on either $x$
or $y$, since both belong to $\operatorname{FOLLOW}(A)$. After
shifting $a c$, try $y$. The probe pops one state, exposing the
state after $a$, then follows its goto for $A$. That state expects
$x$, so the probe returns false. The probe with $x$ starts from
the original stack, takes the same reduction and goto, and returns
true at the shift. The expected set is therefore $\{x\}$, not
$\{x,y\}$. This does not require changing the parse table.

The scratch stack at each step matches a real parser's state stack
after the same reductions, provided semantic actions cannot change
table decisions or the classification of the proposed token. An
empty production pushes a goto after popping nothing; even this
transition cannot be skipped. If a table may cycle, a bounded probe
should report an unknown answer when its budget expires. Enumerating
the expected set costs one copy and one reduction walk per candidate;
a persistent stack can avoid copying the untouched prefix for each
walk.
